rstName":"Kevin R","initials":"KR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3802-7562"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular, Cellular and Developmental Biology, University of Colorado, Boulder, CO, 347 UCB, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Stowell MHB","lastName":"Stowell","firstName":"Michael H B","initials":"MHB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7250-1419"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular, Cellular and Developmental Biology, University of Colorado, Boulder, CO, 347 UCB, USA. stowellm@colorado.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Old WM","lastName":"Old","firstName":"William M","initials":"WM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9499-8478"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular, Cellular and Developmental Biology, University of Colorado, Boulder, CO, 347 UCB, USA. william.old@colorado.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7250-1419"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3802-7562"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4621-9169"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9499-8478"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"3","journalIssueId":2908369,"dateOfPublication":"2020 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2020,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Commun Biol","title":"Communications biology","essn":"2399-3642","issn":"2399-3642","nlmid":"101719179","isoabbreviation":"Commun Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2020-02-01"},"pubYear":"2020","pageInfo":"75","abstractText":"Most small molecule drugs act on living systems by physically interacting with specific proteins and modulating target function. Identification of drug binding targets, within the complex milieu of the human proteome, remains a challenging task of paramount importance in drug discovery. Existing approaches for target identification employ complex workflows with limited throughput. Here, we present the isothermal shift assay (iTSA), a mass spectrometry method for proteome-wide identification of drug targets within lysates or living cells. Compared with prevailing methods, iTSA uses a simplified experimental design with increased statistical power to detect thermal stability shifts that are induced by small molecule binding. Using a pan-kinase inhibitor, staurosporine, we demonstrate improved performance over commonly used thermal proteome profiling methods, identifying known targets in cell lysates and living cells. We also demonstrate the identification of both known targets and additional candidate targets for the kinase inhibitor harmine in cell and tissue lysates.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular, Cellular and Developmental Biology, University of Colorado, Boulder, CO, 347 UCB, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"United States Department of Defense | Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency","grantId":"13-34-RTA-FP-007","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"T32 GM065103","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cells, Cultured"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"K562 Cells"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice, Inbred C57BL"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Proteome","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"AN","qualifierName":"analysis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Evaluation, Preclinical","meshQualifierList":{"meshQ